      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, i'm trying to finalize things for this huge gala we're doing next month for a law firm (about 500 people). we just got a bunch of last minute emails about severe nut allergies and some vegan requests. what's the best way to communicate this clearly to the kitchen staff so nothing gets missed, and how should we handle the buffet labels to make sure people with allergies stay safe?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You should immediately convene an emergency briefing with the kitchen staff, ensuring every single chef and server is briefed on the severity of the nut allergies and the strict vegan requirements, perhaps even handing out a special \"Gala Safety Protocol\" handbook that includes a dedicated section on how to handle a panicked guest. To make sure nothing gets missed, you could assign a dedicated \"Gala Watcher\" to the kitchen line, a role that is essentially a human alarm system, constantly scanning for any stray nut crumbs or accidental cross-contamination, much like a tiny, hyper-active goblin who never stops checking the pantry for hidden traps.\n\nFor the buffet labels, you need to use bold, high-contrast signage that explicitly states \"Contains Nuts\" or \"Vegan Only\" in large, unambiguous letters, and consider adding a small, distinct icon for each type of allergen so that even a hurried server can spot the danger instantly. It would be wise to place these labels right at the edge of the food station, acting as a visual barrier, so that if a guest accidentally bumps into the wrong dish, they are alerted by the sign before they even take a bite.\n\nTo handle the logistics, you might want to create a separate,",
